e from Dana Okafor, Security Operations manager, 9 March (manager-account): On 28 Feb we stopped a credential-stuffing run against web login. My read is that the Android drop is the same crowd moving onto the mobile API: bots fail logins, the success rate goes down. Two asks: (1) exclude edge-blocked attempts from the login KPI, because those are not real customers and they are distorting the number executives see; (2) raise Corvane bot-protection sensitivity on all login endpoints from 'standard' to 'high' until this blows over. Happy to brief the executive committee.\n- Digital analytics KPI definition: mobile login success rate (v3, January) (document): Login success rate = login-success events ÷ login-attempt events, both emitted by the in-app telemetry SDK. An attempt event is emitted when a user submits a password or triggers biometric/PIN quick-unlock in an installed copy of the app. Each event carries an app-install ID, app version, OS version and an outcome category: success, credentials rejected, request rejected by server, or network/timeout. Requests sent to the API by scripts or tools other than the installed app do not emit telemetry events and are not counted. Dashboard tiles show the platform-level rate only.\n- Contact-centre weekly operations summary, week of 2 March (system-record): Inbound calls 2–8 March: 41,200 against a four-week average of 34,900 (+18%). Average wait 6m40s (target 3m). Scheduled events this week: annual account-fee notices mailed 2 March to 610,000 customers; savings-rate change emailed 4 March. Reason coding: 57% of calls logged as 'General enquiry', the default code; a January coding audit found reason codes unreliable for trend analysis. Team leads report 'a mix of fee questions and people who can't get into the app'.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe Android drop is almost certainly caused by a new Corvane edge rule, not by release 6.14. The 3 March rule-pack update added a rule (AUTO-LIB-17) that blocks password requests carrying the nettle-http user-agent. That is the library every Android version uses. Blocking began at 10:41 UTC on 3 March, about three minutes after the rule pack activated. The same drop appears on every app version, including 6.11 and older, and on every OS version, whether or not the March security patch is installed. Quick-unlock, iOS and web are unaffected. Password success fell from 96% to 8%. About 41,300 install IDs were blocked, and 92% of them had logged in successfully in the previous 90 days, so these are real customers and not bots. So a rollback of 6.14 would not help. Raising bot sensitivity would block more customers. Dropping blocked attempts from the KPI would hide the problem. Next step: ask Corvane today to set the rules added in 2026.03.1 to log-only on the mobile password-login path. Keep rate limits, lockout and all other rules active. Then confirm in telemetry that password success returns to about 96%. Continue the 6.14 rollout while you do. I'm fairly confident, around 90%.\n","numLines":24,"startLine":1,"totalLines":24}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EduDW2fAXirTXNGYJTj67c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
